-- PARTICLES: Visual Effects That Make the Game Feel Alive
-- This file manages two particle systems: general particles and fire particles.
-- Particles are small shapes that move, shrink, and fade away over time.
-- They add visual polish but have no effect on gameplay.


-- SECTION: Particle State Variables

-- VARIABLE: particles
-- TYPE: List of particle objects
-- PURPOSE: Stores all active general particles (death explosions, coin sparkles, movement dust).
-- Each particle has: x, y, dx, dy, life, decay, size, color.
-- The list grows when new particles spawn and shrinks as particles die.

-- VARIABLE: fireParticles
-- TYPE: List of fire particle objects
-- PURPOSE: Stores particles that float upward from fire hazards.
-- These are separate because they use different rendering (circles vs squares)
-- and different physics (no gravity, circular shape).


-- SECTION: Spawning Particles

-- FUNCTION: spawnParticle
-- PARAMETERS: x (position), y (position), type (what kind of event triggered this)
-- PURPOSE: Creates a burst of particles at a location. Used for deaths, coin pickups, and movement.
--
-- CONDITIONAL: if type is "death" then set count to 20.
-- CONDITIONAL: if type is "coin" then set count to 8.
-- Otherwise set count to 3.
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: Death creates the most particles (big explosion),
-- coins create a medium burst (satisfying feedback), and movement creates few (subtle dust).
--
-- count to the particle count and when you are done create one particle:
--   For each particle, randomize its properties:
--
--   set x to the spawn position x.
--   set y to the spawn position y.
--
--   set dx to a random number between -0.5 and 0.5.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if type is "death" then multiply range by 10 (spread = -5 to 5).
--   -- Otherwise multiply range by 4 (spread = -2 to 2).
--   -- Death particles fly outward faster and farther.
--
--   set dy to a random number between -0.5 and 0.5, minus 2.
--   -- The "minus 2" bias makes particles tend to float upward initially.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if death then multiply by 10 for explosive spread.
--
--   set life to 1.
--   -- Life starts at 1 (fully alive) and decreases toward 0 (dead).
--   -- This value is also used as the particle's opacity (transparency).
--
--   set decay to a random number between 0.02 and 0.04.
--   -- Each frame, life decreases by this amount. Random decay means
--   -- particles disappear at slightly different times, looking more natural.
--
--   set size to a random number.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if death then 4 to 8 pixels. Otherwise 3 to 5 pixels.
--
--   set color based on type.
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if death then "#ff4400" (orange-red, like fire).
--   -- CONDITIONAL: if coin then "#ffcc00" (gold, like coins).
--   -- Otherwise "#00ffcc" (cyan, like the player's trail).


-- SECTION: Updating Fire Particles

-- FUNCTION: updateFireParticles
-- PARAMETERS: fires (the list of fire hazards)
-- PURPOSE: Creates new fire particles and updates existing ones each frame.
--
-- FOR EACH fire hazard in the fires list:
--   CONDITIONAL: if a random number is less than 0.4 (40 percent chance per frame) then
--     Create a new fire particle:
--       set x to a random position within the fire's width.
--       set y to the top of the fire.
--       set dy to negative 1 to negative 3 (floats upward).
--       set dx to a tiny random horizontal drift.
--       set life to 1.
--       set size to 3 to 8 pixels.
--   -- This creates a flickering, dynamic fire effect.
--   -- Not every fire spawns a particle every frame (40 percent chance), which adds randomness.
--
-- THEN update all existing fire particles:
-- Loop BACKWARD through the fire particles list:
--   -- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: We loop backward because we might remove items.
--   -- Removing from the end of a list is safe during backward iteration.
--   -- Removing during forward iteration would skip elements.
--
--   Move the particle: add dy to y, add dx to x.
--   Reduce life by 0.03 each frame.
--   Shrink size by multiplying by 0.97 (3 percent smaller each frame).
--
--   CONDITIONAL: if life is less than or equal to 0 then remove this particle.
--   -- REMOVAL TRICK: Instead of expensive array splice, swap with the last element and pop.
--   -- This is an O(1) removal technique (instant) vs O(n) splice (slow for large lists).
--   -- The swap-and-pop works because particle order does not matter for rendering.


-- SECTION: Updating General Particles

-- FUNCTION: updateParticles
-- PURPOSE: Moves and ages all general particles each frame.
--
-- Loop BACKWARD through the particles list:
--   Move: add dx to x, add dy to y.
--   Apply gravity: add 0.2 to dy.
--   -- Unlike fire particles, general particles fall downward due to gravity.
--   -- This makes death explosions arc naturally like debris.
--   Reduce life by the particle's decay rate.
--
--   CONDITIONAL: if life is 0 or below then remove using swap-and-pop.


-- SECTION: Drawing Particles

-- FUNCTION: drawParticles
-- PARAMETERS: ctx (the canvas rendering context)
-- PURPOSE: Renders all general particles as colored squares.
-- FOR EACH particle:
--   Set fill color to the particle's color.
--   Set global alpha (transparency) to the particle's life value.
--   -- As life goes from 1 to 0, the particle fades from fully visible to invisible.
--   Draw a filled rectangle centered on the particle's position.
-- After drawing all particles, reset global alpha to 1 (fully opaque).
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: Forgetting to reset globalAlpha would make everything else transparent too.

-- FUNCTION: drawFireParticles
-- PARAMETERS: ctx (the canvas rendering context)
-- PURPOSE: Renders fire particles as glowing orange circles.
-- FOR EACH fire particle:
--   Set fill color using rgba with the life value controlling both green channel and alpha.
--   -- rgba(255, 100 + life*100, 0, life) creates colors ranging from
--   -- bright yellow-orange (alive) to dim red-orange (dying).
--   Draw a circle using arc at the particle's position with its current size as radius.
-- EDUCATIONAL NOTE: Fire particles use circles (arc) while general particles use squares (fillRect).
-- This visual difference makes fire effects look softer and more organic.


-- SECTION: Cleanup

-- FUNCTION: clearParticles
-- PURPOSE: Removes ALL particles from both systems.
-- Sets the particles list to an empty list.
-- Sets the fireParticles list to an empty list.
-- Called when loading a new level or restarting the game.
-- This prevents particles from the previous level lingering in the new one.
